  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cold front sinks south Thursday and Friday, bringing \ngradually improving conditions with high pressure overhead Saturday\nand the first half of Sunday.\n\nThe stationary front that has lingered across the region the\nlast few days will slowly sink south as a cold front Thursday \nand Friday, allowing gradually improving conditions to filter\nin. Aside from a few showers in the morning, zonal flow aloft \nwill keep Thursday largely dry, though cooler temperatures and \nlower humidity will not arrive until Friday and especially \nSaturday. A few showers will remain possible Friday night as the\ncold front finally shifts far south enough to allow surface \nhigh pressure to be overhead Saturday and into early Sunday. The\nforecast Saturday looks to be the driest, coolest, and least \nhumid day through the weekend with highs in the upper 70s to mid\n80s and dew points only reaching the low to mid 60s. High \npressure will not last, however, and shift offshore Sunday, \nbringing return flow back across the region, allowing \ntemperatures to creep back up into the mid to upper 80s and the \nhumidity to return. The morning will be largely dry with chances\nfor rain returning by the afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather returns Sunday afternoon into\nMonday.\n\nThe next storm system arrives Sunday afternoon into Monday as a\nsurface low moves across the Great Lakes, Ohio Valley, and\neventually the Mid Atlantic, bringing the chance for showers and\nthunderstorms. Global guidance shows PWAT values surging back\ninto the 2\" range, increasing the potential for heavy downpours\nwithin any thunderstorms that develop. As a result, WPC does\nhave much of the Northeast and Mid Atlantic in a Marginal risk\nin the Excessive Rainfall Outlook. Furthermore, some modest \ninstability may be in place to support a severe potential. That \nsaid, the overall synoptic level forcing is limited with the \nmain driving force being a weak upper level trough. At this \npoint, it remains too early to lock down any specifics for\nsevere and flash flood, but we will need to monitor the\npotential over the next several days.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Spotty minor tidal flooding possible during the\nThursday evening high tide along the Atlantic coast, Delaware \nBay, and tidal Delaware River.\n\nWith the New Moon having occurred yesterday, astronomical \ntides for this evening's high tide cycle will be near minor \nflooding thresholds for many places along the Atlantic coasts of\nNew Jersey and Delware, Delaware Bay, and the tidal Delaware \nRiver, though tides are expected to begin trending downward as\nwe move away from the New Moon. As of the latest forecast, only\nspotty minor flooding is possible with this evening's high \ntide.\n\nTidal flooding is not currently forecast for the Eastern Shore of \nthe Chesapeake Bay.",
